==Introduction==

===Abstract===

This document proposes a family of codex32 human-readable parts (HRPs) for the key material that appears in a
[https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0380.mediawiki BIP-0380] descriptor <code>KEY</code> expression:
public keys, x-only public keys, WIF-equivalent private keys, and BIP-0032 extended keys. It depends on
[https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/pull/2040 the codex32 HRP generalization], since each type below needs its own HRP
rather than the fixed "ms" BIP-0093 currently requires.

This is a concept-stage draft: naming and two specific open questions (below) are unresolved, and no test vectors
have been written yet. It is being circulated for feedback before either is finalized.

===Copyright===

This document is licensed under the 3-clause BSD license.

===Motivation===

A descriptor's <code>KEY</code> expression can be a hex public key, a WIF private key, or a base58 xpub/xprv, any of
which may need to survive being copied by hand, printed, or transmitted through a channel (QR, voice, a paper card)
prone to transcription errors. None of these formats has BIP-0093's checksum, error-detection/-correction, or
Shamir-secret-sharing properties: a single mistyped character in an xpub either fails opaquely or, worse, silently
resolves to a different valid xpub. Extending codex32 to this material gives it the same guarantees a codex32 seed
backup already has.

==Specification (sketch)==

===HRP registry===

{| class="wikitable"
! HRP !! Meaning !! Payload
|-
| <code>pk</code> || Public key || 33 bytes (compressed) or 65 bytes (uncompressed)
|-
| <code>xo</code> || X-only public key (BIP-0340) || 32 bytes
|-
| <code>wif</code> / <code>twif</code> || Private key, mainnet / testnet || 32-byte scalar plus a compression-status marker (form undecided; see Open questions)
|-
| <code>xpub</code> / <code>xprv</code> / <code>tpub</code> / <code>tprv</code> || BIP-0032 extended key, mainnet/testnet public/private || depth, parent fingerprint, child number, chain code, key (74 bytes; the codex32 HRP replaces the base58 version bytes)
|-
| ''undecided'' (<code>mn</code> or <code>rn</code>) || Combined chain code + private key ("root node"), for a BIP-0032 master key with no depth/fingerprint/child-number fields || 64 bytes
|}

<code>pk</code> and <code>xo</code> deliberately have no network-tagged variant, matching BIP-0380's own hex pubkeys:
the key material is network-agnostic, and network is a property of the address/script built from it, not the key.

An xpub or xprv's own fingerprint (first 32 bits of Hash160 of its pubkey) MAY be used as its codex32 identifier by
default only for HRPs whose fingerprint carries no separate correlation risk (<code>xo</code>). For <code>pk</code>,
the fingerprint doubles as the P2PKH/P2WPKH address's own hash160, so using it as a share identifier would let a
single below-threshold share leak which on-chain address(es) it belongs to; other HRPs should not default to it
either without the same analysis.

===Key-origin information===

Open question: encode <code>[fingerprint/path]</code> exactly as BIP-0380 does, as a bracketed prefix outside the
codex32 string, or offer folding it into the codex32 HRP itself? See Open questions.

===Network and compression status, measured against WIF===

Wallet Import Format already solves both problems, and sets the bar this document should meet. WIF base58check-encodes
a version byte (<code>0x80</code> mainnet, <code>0xEF</code> testnet), the 32-byte scalar, and an appended
<code>0x01</code> when the key belongs to a compressed public key. Because base58's leading character follows from the
leading bytes' numeric range, both facts fall out of the first character:

{| class="wikitable"
! !! uncompressed !! compressed
|-
| '''mainnet''' || <code>5</code>, 51 characters || <code>K</code> or <code>L</code>, 52 characters
|-
| '''testnet''' || <code>9</code>, 51 characters || <code>c</code>, 52 characters
|}

So WIF spends one character on compression status (the appended byte costs one base58 character) and gets
glanceability for both fields as a side effect of the encoding, not as a deliberate allocation.

Codex32 already exceeds WIF on network: <code>wif</code> versus <code>twif</code> is a human-readable prefix rather
than a leading character whose meaning has to be memorised. Compression status is the open question, and a 32-byte
scalar occupies 52 symbols with exactly 4 spare bits, which gives three candidates:

# '''Dedicated leading symbol.''' Reserve two charset values at a fixed position, for example <code>q</code>
  (value 0) for compressed and <code>p</code> (value 1) for uncompressed, giving the common case the simplest symbol
  as segwit did for witness version 0. Costs one symbol, 53 payload symbols in total. A single fixed character per
  state, so marginally clearer than WIF's <code>K</code>/<code>L</code> pair. '''Matches WIF's cost.'''
# '''Trailing spare bits.''' Put the flag in the payload's existing 4 spare bits. Costs nothing, 52 payload symbols,
  but the flag shares its symbol with key data, so the character looks random and the status is invisible without
  decoding — exactly the weakness of WIF's internal <code>0x01</code> byte, without base58's accidental compensation.
  '''Cheaper than WIF, less readable.'''
# '''Leading spare bits.''' Place the same 4 spare bits at the front instead of the back. The first payload symbol is
  then 4 flag bits plus one key bit, so it takes exactly two values per state: compressed (<code>0b0000</code>) makes
  it <code>q</code> or <code>p</code>, uncompressed (<code>0b0001</code>) makes it <code>z</code> or <code>r</code>.
  Costs nothing, 52 payload symbols, and is glanceable at a two-character pair — the same cognitive load as WIF's
  <code>K</code>/<code>L</code>. '''Exceeds WIF: same readability, one character cheaper.'''

Option 3 spends the spare bits that would otherwise be available for a padding CRC; they can carry a flag or a
checksum, not both. That trade is acceptable for <code>wif</code>/<code>twif</code> specifically, because a private
scalar has no curve-membership constraint and so does not depend on padding being computed rather than chosen — see
Rationale. A fourth option is to decline uncompressed private keys altogether and spend nothing, at the cost of being
unable to represent legacy keys that BIP-0380 still permits in <code>pk()</code>, <code>pkh()</code> and
<code>combo()</code>.

Spare-bit inventory for the other types, for reviewers weighing whether the same technique applies elsewhere:
compressed <code>pk</code> has 1 spare bit, <code>xo</code> has 4, the extended-key body has 3, the root node has 3,
and uncompressed <code>pk</code> has none. Only <code>wif</code> currently has a status worth signalling; the rest are
single-form, or already distinguished by length (<code>pk</code>, at 76 versus 129 characters) or by the HRP itself.

==Rationale==

'''Why <code>pk</code> and not <code>pub</code>?''' Consistency with the descriptor function names that already mean
"public key" everywhere in BIP-0380/381/382 (<code>pk()</code>, <code>pkh()</code>, <code>wpkh()</code>), and it's
shorter. It reads like "private key" out of context, but not inside a codex32/descriptor context where every use of
"pk" already means public key.

'''Why not shorten <code>wif</code>/<code>twif</code> further?''' "WIF" is already a maximally-compressed,
universally-recognized term with no equally-recognized shorter form to fall back on, unlike <code>pub</code>→<code>pk</code>
which had one ready to reuse. <code>twf</code>, <code>twi</code>, and <code>wit</code> (the last easily confused with
"witness") are all less recognizable than keeping the existing name with a <code>t</code> prefix, matching
<code>tpub</code>/<code>tprv</code>.

'''Why does <code>pk</code>/<code>xo</code>/<code>xpub</code>/<code>tpub</code> generation require padding rather
than free choice, unlike a seed?''' These payloads embed an actual elliptic-curve public key, which must satisfy
curve membership; a randomly chosen payload essentially never does. BIP-0093's "fresh secret" procedure (freely
choose payload symbols) therefore doesn't apply to these HRPs the way it does to a seed — implementations MUST treat
the last symbol(s) as fixed padding rather than free choice for these types. This does not apply to
<code>xprv</code>/<code>tprv</code>/<code>wif</code>/<code>twif</code>: a private scalar has no membership
constraint, so free choice remains valid there.

==Backwards compatibility==

New HRPs under the generalized-HRP codex32 grammar; no effect on existing "ms"/"cl"-HRP codex32 strings or on
BIP-0380 descriptors that don't use codex32-encoded keys.

==Open questions==

* Key-origin info: bracketed BIP-0380 syntax outside the codex32 string, or an HRP-folded form? Folding it in is a
  real QR-size reduction for multisig descriptors (origin info is often the largest contributor to a cosigner-heavy
  descriptor's QR footprint), at the cost of a second way to write the same information.
* WIF compression status: which of the four options in "Network and compression status, measured against WIF" to
  adopt. Leading spare bits (option 3) appear to beat WIF outright — same two-character readability, one character
  cheaper than a dedicated symbol — but they consume the bits a padding CRC would otherwise use, and they put
  meaningful bits at the front of a payload where codex32 conventionally pads at the end. Reviewers who consider that
  deviation unacceptable should say so, in which case option 1 matches WIF rather than exceeding it.
* Root-node HRP name: <code>mn</code> ("master node") or <code>rn</code> ("root node")? <code>rn</code> is
  unregistered in SLIP-0173 as of this writing; <code>mn</code> needs the same check.
* Whether/how to specify folding origin info into a multisig descriptor's overall QR encoding at the Bitcoin Core
  level (an export-xpub feature), versus leaving that entirely to wallet software. Any such change is purely
  encoder-side: a standard QR reader decodes a multi-segment code identically to a single-segment one, so this
  imposes no requirement on decoders.

